Plan: Deploy RAG Application to AWS ECS Fargate
TL;DR
Deploy your Node.js RAG app to AWS ECS Fargate (managed containers) with a REST API, backed by RDS PostgreSQL + pgvector, and Ollama on a separate EC2 instance (or switch to AWS Bedrock). Covers full AWS infrastructure setup, code changes, containerization, and deployment.

Steps (8 Phases)
Phase 1: AWS Infrastructure Setup

Create VPC, subnets, Internet Gateway, NAT Gateway
Create Security Groups (Ollama, RDS, Fargate, ALB)
Create RDS PostgreSQL with pgvector extension
Create IAM roles for ECS & EC2
Create Application Load Balancer (ALB)
Phase 2: Code Modifications
6. Update config to use environment variables (not hardcoded)
7. Convert rag.js to Express.js REST API with POST /api/ask and GET /health endpoints
8. Create Dockerfile for Node.js app
9. Create docker-compose-aws.yml for local testing

Phase 3: Container Registry
10. Create AWS ECR repository
11. Build & push Docker image to ECR

Phase 4: Ollama Deployment (Choose A or B)

Option A (EC2): Launch Ubuntu instance, install Ollama, pull models
Option B (Bedrock): Use AWS Bedrock API instead (simpler, serverless)
Phase 5: ECS Cluster & Service
14. Create ECS cluster with Fargate
15. Create ECS task definition pointing to ECR image + RDS/Ollama config
16. Create ECS service with 2+ tasks, attach to ALB

Phase 6: Database Initialization
17. Enable pgvector extension in RDS, create tables
18. Seed initial documents (policy.txt, annuity.txt) via seeding script

Phase 7: Testing & Validation
19. Local Docker testing with docker-compose
20. Deploy to ECS, test health endpoint
21. End-to-end test: Question → Embedding → Search → LLM → Answer

Phase 8: Monitoring
22. Set up CloudWatch dashboards & alarms
23. Configure auto-scaling (optional)
24. Set up CI/CD pipeline (optional)

